1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:06,037
In the spring of 1900, a boat full of sponge divers was running for cover.

2
00:00:06,087 --> 00:00:11,517
A storm had pushed them off course, and they sheltered behind a small rocky island

3
00:00:11,567 --> 00:00:14,095
between Crete and mainland Greece.

4
00:00:14,145 --> 00:00:16,094
Its name was Antikythera.

5
00:00:16,894 --> 00:00:19,360
While they waited for the weather to turn,

6
00:00:19,410 --> 00:00:22,379
one of the divers went down to look for sponges.

7
00:00:22,429 --> 00:00:26,739
He came back up badly shaken, saying the seabed was covered in bodies.

8
00:00:27,539 --> 00:00:28,956
They weren't bodies.

9
00:00:29,006 --> 00:00:33,640
They were bronze and marble statues, lying on the wreck of a cargo ship that had

10
00:00:33,690 --> 00:00:36,405
gone down about two thousand years earlier.

11
00:00:37,205 --> 00:00:40,229
Over the next year, divers hauled up statues,

12
00:00:40,279 --> 00:00:42,504
pottery, glassware and coins.

13
00:00:42,554 --> 00:00:45,701
And among all of it, a lump of corroded bronze,

14
00:00:45,751 --> 00:00:50,251
roughly the size of a shoebox, that nobody paid much attention to.

15
00:00:51,051 --> 00:00:52,945
Inside that lump were gears.

16
00:00:52,995 --> 00:00:57,062
Dozens of them, some with teeth barely more than a millimetre long,

17
00:00:57,112 --> 00:00:59,978
cut by hand more than two thousand years ago.

18
00:01:00,778 --> 00:01:03,405
It is a machine for calculating the sky.

19
00:01:03,455 --> 00:01:08,060
Turn a handle, and it shows you where the Sun and Moon will be on any day you

20
00:01:08,110 --> 00:01:10,620
choose, what phase the Moon will be in,

21
00:01:10,670 --> 00:01:13,239
and which months will bring an eclipse.

22
00:01:13,739 --> 00:01:18,328
Nothing with this kind of gearing survives from anywhere in the world for more than

23
00:01:18,378 --> 00:01:20,205
a thousand years afterwards.

24
00:01:20,255 --> 00:01:22,966
This is how it worked, one gear at a time.

25
00:01:28,566 --> 00:01:32,644
The finds went to the National Archaeological Museum in Athens,

26
00:01:32,694 --> 00:01:35,271
where the statues got the attention.

27
00:01:35,321 --> 00:01:38,961
The bronze lump dried out on its own, and as it dried,

28
00:01:39,011 --> 00:01:40,400
it cracked apart.

29
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:46,927
On the seventeenth of May, 1902, the archaeologist Valerios Stais looked closely at

30
00:01:46,977 --> 00:01:50,669
one of the pieces and saw a gear wheel embedded in it,

31
00:01:50,719 --> 00:01:53,032
along with tiny Greek letters.

32
00:01:53,832 --> 00:01:57,262
Today the mechanism exists as eighty two fragments.

33
00:01:57,312 --> 00:02:00,437
Perhaps a third of the original machine survives,

34
00:02:00,487 --> 00:02:05,505
including thirty bronze gears, and the largest fragment holds most of them.

35
00:02:06,305 --> 00:02:11,138
The problem was that almost everything important was locked inside the corrosion.

36
00:02:11,188 --> 00:02:13,495
You could see the edge of a wheel here,

37
00:02:13,545 --> 00:02:16,862
a few letters there, but not how anything connected.

38
00:02:18,212 --> 00:02:23,313
The first person to make real progress was a British physicist and historian of

39
00:02:23,363 --> 00:02:25,760
science, Derek de Solla Price.

40
00:02:25,810 --> 00:02:30,331
In 1971 he worked with the Greek radiographer Charalambos Karakalos,

41
00:02:30,381 --> 00:02:35,804
who X-rayed the fragments, and together they began counting the teeth on the gears

42
00:02:35,854 --> 00:02:37,156
they could see.

43
00:02:37,956 --> 00:02:40,490
Counting teeth sounds like a small thing,

44
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:44,366
but in a geared machine, the tooth counts are the program.

45
00:02:44,416 --> 00:02:47,655
They tell you exactly what ratio each wheel turns at,

46
00:02:47,705 --> 00:02:50,768
and so what the machine was built to calculate.

47
00:02:51,568 --> 00:02:54,686
One gear had about a hundred and twenty seven teeth,

48
00:02:54,736 --> 00:02:56,933
which is an odd number to choose.

49
00:02:56,983 --> 00:03:01,887
Price realised that twice a hundred and twenty seven is two hundred and fifty four,

50
00:03:01,937 --> 00:03:06,149
and two hundred and fifty four is a number astronomers already knew.

51
00:03:06,199 --> 00:03:07,992
We'll see why in a moment.

52
00:03:08,792 --> 00:03:11,044
The breakthrough came in 2005.

53
00:03:11,094 --> 00:03:16,417
A team shipped a specialised X-ray scanner from England to the museum in Athens and

54
00:03:16,467 --> 00:03:19,550
scanned every fragment in three dimensions.

55
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:25,178
For the first time, researchers could look through the corrosion layer by layer.

56
00:03:25,978 --> 00:03:29,927
The scans revealed gears hidden deep inside the largest fragment,

57
00:03:29,977 --> 00:03:35,161
and thousands of characters of text that had been invisible for two thousand years.

58
00:03:35,211 --> 00:03:39,219
Much of that text turned out to be something like a user's manual,

59
00:03:39,269 --> 00:03:41,807
describing what the machine displayed.

60
00:03:43,157 --> 00:03:45,835
To understand what the mechanism calculates,

61
00:03:45,885 --> 00:03:48,272
you need the problem it was solving.

62
00:03:48,322 --> 00:03:52,277
Seen from Earth, the Sun drifts around a band of constellations,

63
00:03:52,327 --> 00:03:54,134
the zodiac, once a year.

64
00:03:54,934 --> 00:03:57,599
The Moon travels the same band much faster,

65
00:03:57,649 --> 00:04:01,495
going all the way around in a little over twenty seven days.

66
00:04:02,295 --> 00:04:06,021
But the cycle people actually lived by was the Moon's phases,

67
00:04:06,071 --> 00:04:07,614
new moon to new moon.

68
00:04:07,664 --> 00:04:10,623
That takes about twenty nine and a half days.

69
00:04:10,673 --> 00:04:13,751
Ancient Greek calendars ran on these lunar months,

70
00:04:13,801 --> 00:04:15,934
and festivals were set by them.

71
00:04:16,734 --> 00:04:18,117
Here's the trouble.

72
00:04:18,167 --> 00:04:21,386
A year doesn't hold a whole number of lunar months.

73
00:04:21,436 --> 00:04:23,679
It holds about twelve and a third.

74
00:04:23,729 --> 00:04:28,381
So a calendar of twelve lunar months drifts away from the seasons by about eleven

75
00:04:28,431 --> 00:04:29,642
days every year.

76
00:04:30,442 --> 00:04:35,099
Astronomers found a way out, known to the Greeks as the Metonic cycle.

77
00:04:35,149 --> 00:04:40,228
Nineteen years contain almost exactly two hundred and thirty five lunar months.

78
00:04:40,278 --> 00:04:43,607
The mismatch is about two hours in nineteen years.

79
00:04:43,657 --> 00:04:47,891
So if you add seven extra months spread across those nineteen years,

80
00:04:47,941 --> 00:04:50,908
the calendar stays in step with the seasons.

81
00:04:51,708 --> 00:04:54,867
And now two hundred and fifty four makes sense.

82
00:04:54,917 --> 00:05:00,134
In those same nineteen years, the Moon goes around the zodiac two hundred and thirty

83
00:05:00,184 --> 00:05:04,190
five times plus nineteen, once for every lap the Sun makes.

84
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:06,188
Two hundred and fifty four.

85
00:05:07,538 --> 00:05:10,029
A pair of meshing gears multiplies.

86
00:05:10,079 --> 00:05:14,059
If a gear with sixty four teeth drives a gear with thirty two,

87
00:05:14,109 --> 00:05:17,902
the small one turns twice for every turn of the big one.

88
00:05:17,952 --> 00:05:21,125
The ratio is just the tooth counts, divided.

89
00:05:21,925 --> 00:05:25,632
Chain several pairs together, and the ratios multiply.

90
00:05:25,682 --> 00:05:29,147
In the mechanism, the main wheel turns once per year,

91
00:05:29,197 --> 00:05:30,784
driven by the handle.

92
00:05:30,834 --> 00:05:34,480
From it, a train of gears runs to the Moon's pointer.

93
00:05:35,280 --> 00:05:37,307
Sixty four over thirty eight.

94
00:05:37,357 --> 00:05:39,384
Forty eight over twenty four.

95
00:05:39,434 --> 00:05:42,291
A hundred and twenty seven over thirty two.

96
00:05:42,341 --> 00:05:47,513
Multiply them out, and you get exactly two hundred and fifty four over nineteen.

97
00:05:48,313 --> 00:05:53,171
One turn of the year wheel moves the Moon pointer through two hundred and fifty four

98
00:05:53,221 --> 00:05:54,788
nineteenths of a turn.

99
00:05:54,838 --> 00:05:57,445
The Moon laps the zodiac at the right rate,

100
00:05:57,495 --> 00:06:01,718
forever, with no arithmetic done by the person turning the handle.

101
00:06:01,768 --> 00:06:03,912
The arithmetic is in the bronze.

102
00:06:05,262 --> 00:06:07,746
The Moon has one more complication.

103
00:06:07,796 --> 00:06:10,590
Its speed across the sky isn't constant.

104
00:06:10,640 --> 00:06:13,248
Over about twenty seven and a half days,

105
00:06:13,298 --> 00:06:17,080
it speeds up, then slows down, then speeds up again.

106
00:06:17,880 --> 00:06:19,167
We now know why.

107
00:06:19,217 --> 00:06:24,393
The Moon's orbit is an ellipse, and it moves faster when it's closer to Earth.

108
00:06:24,443 --> 00:06:27,005
The Greeks didn't have that explanation,

109
00:06:27,055 --> 00:06:30,712
but the astronomer Hipparchus, in the second century BC,

110
00:06:30,762 --> 00:06:34,176
built a geometric model that reproduced the effect.

111
00:06:34,976 --> 00:06:37,919
The mechanism builds that model in metal.

112
00:06:37,969 --> 00:06:42,823
Two gears of fifty teeth each sit on axes offset by about a millimetre.

113
00:06:42,873 --> 00:06:46,198
A pin on one gear rides in a slot on the other.

114
00:06:46,248 --> 00:06:50,019
As they turn, the pin slides in and out along the slot,

115
00:06:50,069 --> 00:06:52,757
and the second gear runs slightly ahead,

116
00:06:52,807 --> 00:06:54,476
then slightly behind.

117
00:06:55,276 --> 00:06:58,696
The result is a Moon pointer that speeds up and slows down,

118
00:06:58,746 --> 00:07:00,543
the way the real Moon does.

119
00:07:01,343 --> 00:07:06,686
And the high and low points of the Moon's speed don't stay in one place in the sky.

120
00:07:06,736 --> 00:07:10,504
They creep around the zodiac about once every nine years.

121
00:07:10,554 --> 00:07:15,534
So the whole pin and slot assembly is mounted on a larger gear that slowly carries

122
00:07:15,584 --> 00:07:18,200
it around, once in about nine years.

123
00:07:18,250 --> 00:07:22,018
It is a moving mechanism riding on another moving mechanism,

124
00:07:22,068 --> 00:07:24,684
made more than two thousand years ago.

125
00:07:26,034 --> 00:07:29,773
Turn the box around, and the back has two large dials,

126
00:07:29,823 --> 00:07:31,828
each laid out as a spiral.

127
00:07:31,878 --> 00:07:34,011
The upper one is a calendar.

128
00:07:34,061 --> 00:07:39,406
Its pointer travels five turns of a spiral divided into two hundred and thirty five

129
00:07:39,456 --> 00:07:41,974
cells, one cell per lunar month.

130
00:07:42,024 --> 00:07:43,901
The Metonic cycle again.

131
00:07:44,701 --> 00:07:47,984
A pin at the pointer's tip rides in the spiral's groove,

132
00:07:48,034 --> 00:07:51,318
so the pointer lengthens as it works its way outward.

133
00:07:51,368 --> 00:07:54,878
When it reaches the end, you lift it back to the start.

134
00:07:55,678 --> 00:07:58,462
The inscribed month names are Corinthian,

135
00:07:58,512 --> 00:08:02,843
which hints at where the machine was made or meant to be used.

136
00:08:02,893 --> 00:08:07,931
A smaller dial inside the spiral tracks a four year cycle of athletic games,

137
00:08:07,981 --> 00:08:09,799
including the Olympics.

138
00:08:10,599 --> 00:08:13,455
The lower spiral is the most remarkable.

139
00:08:13,505 --> 00:08:17,435
It has four turns and two hundred and twenty three cells.

140
00:08:17,485 --> 00:08:20,278
Two hundred and twenty three lunar months,

141
00:08:20,328 --> 00:08:23,184
about eighteen years, is the Saros cycle,

142
00:08:23,234 --> 00:08:26,153
which Babylonian astronomers had discovered:

143
00:08:26,203 --> 00:08:29,249
after one Saros, eclipses tend to repeat.

144
00:08:30,049 --> 00:08:35,042
Some cells carry small inscriptions, glyphs marking an expected eclipse of the Sun

145
00:08:35,092 --> 00:08:38,008
or the Moon, and even the hour of the day.

146
00:08:38,058 --> 00:08:42,992
Point to this month, and the machine tells you whether to expect an eclipse.

147
00:08:44,342 --> 00:08:48,383
The front of the mechanism had one large dial with two rings.

148
00:08:48,433 --> 00:08:50,520
The inner ring is the zodiac.

149
00:08:50,570 --> 00:08:55,587
The outer ring is the Egyptian calendar of three hundred and sixty five days.

150
00:08:55,637 --> 00:09:00,899
That calendar ignored leap days, so its ring could be turned by hand to stay aligned

151
00:09:00,949 --> 00:09:02,059
with the Sun.

152
00:09:02,859 --> 00:09:07,418
Pointers showed the positions of the Sun and the Moon against the zodiac.

153
00:09:07,468 --> 00:09:10,160
And a small ball, half pale and half dark,

154
00:09:10,210 --> 00:09:12,377
turned to show the Moon's phase.

155
00:09:12,427 --> 00:09:17,045
Its rotation came from a gear arrangement that takes the difference between the

156
00:09:17,095 --> 00:09:19,028
Moon's motion and the Sun's.

157
00:09:19,828 --> 00:09:24,048
The inscriptions also name the five planets known in antiquity:

158
00:09:24,098 --> 00:09:27,478
Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn.

159
00:09:27,528 --> 00:09:32,719
None of the planetary gears survive, so what came next is reconstruction.

160
00:09:33,519 --> 00:09:38,622
In 2021, a team at University College London published a proposed design for the

161
00:09:38,672 --> 00:09:40,670
whole front of the machine.

162
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:44,768
Their starting point was two numbers found in the inscriptions:

163
00:09:44,818 --> 00:09:48,182
four hundred and sixty two, associated with Venus,

164
00:09:48,232 --> 00:09:51,410
and four hundred and forty two, with Saturn.

165
00:09:51,460 --> 00:09:54,949
Both are long, accurate cycles for those planets.

166
00:09:55,749 --> 00:10:00,537
From those cycles they designed gear trains that fit in the space available,

167
00:10:00,587 --> 00:10:03,415
using methods the Greeks could have used.

168
00:10:03,465 --> 00:10:07,701
It shows how the whole known cosmos might have fitted into one box,

169
00:10:07,751 --> 00:10:11,681
although the planetary display itself remains a hypothesis.

170
00:10:13,031 --> 00:10:14,996
We don't know who made it.

171
00:10:15,046 --> 00:10:20,160
The Roman orator Cicero describes a bronze sphere built by Archimedes that showed

172
00:10:20,210 --> 00:10:23,057
the motions of the Sun, Moon and planets,

173
00:10:23,107 --> 00:10:28,347
and he mentions a similar device made by the philosopher Posidonius on Rhodes.

174
00:10:28,397 --> 00:10:30,048
Neither has survived.

175
00:10:30,098 --> 00:10:34,582
The Antikythera mechanism is the only machine of its kind we have.

176
00:10:35,382 --> 00:10:37,853
The ship sank around sixty BC.

177
00:10:37,903 --> 00:10:42,684
Studies of the eclipse dial suggest its cycle starts in 205 BC.

178
00:10:42,734 --> 00:10:48,355
That's when the calendar begins, which is not necessarily when the machine was

179
00:10:48,405 --> 00:10:52,696
built, but it places the design in the second century BC,

180
00:10:52,746 --> 00:10:55,217
around the time of Hipparchus.

181
00:10:56,017 --> 00:10:58,044
And then the trail goes cold.

182
00:10:58,094 --> 00:11:02,910
Gearing of this complexity doesn't appear again in the surviving record until the

183
00:11:02,960 --> 00:11:05,403
astronomical clocks of medieval Europe,

184
00:11:05,453 --> 00:11:07,302
in the fourteenth century.

185
00:11:08,652 --> 00:11:12,785
It's tempting to call it a computer, and in one sense it is.

186
00:11:12,835 --> 00:11:16,168
It takes an input, a date, and computes outputs,

187
00:11:16,218 --> 00:11:18,690
the positions and phases and eclipses,

188
00:11:18,740 --> 00:11:21,150
using nothing but ratios of teeth.

189
00:11:21,650 --> 00:11:23,865
But it's also something simpler.

190
00:11:23,915 --> 00:11:24,997
It's a model.

191
00:11:25,047 --> 00:11:28,931
Somebody took everything their civilisation knew about the sky,

192
00:11:28,981 --> 00:11:33,520
the cycles found by Babylonian astronomers and the geometry of Greek ones,

193
00:11:33,570 --> 00:11:35,963
and made it into a thing you can hold,

194
00:11:36,013 --> 00:11:37,632
and turn, and watch.

195
00:11:38,132 --> 00:11:43,214
In the early 1700s, English instrument makers built a clockwork model of the solar

196
00:11:43,264 --> 00:11:46,051
system, and a copy made for Charles Boyle,

197
00:11:46,101 --> 00:11:49,794
the Earl of Orrery, gave every machine like it a name.

198
00:11:49,844 --> 00:11:52,692
Orreries: working models of the heavens.

199
00:11:52,742 --> 00:11:56,073
The one from Antikythera is the oldest we know of.

200
00:11:56,123 --> 00:12:00,601
That idea, building a model so you can see how a thing actually works,

201
00:12:00,651 --> 00:12:02,775
is what this channel is about.
